            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Charles de Gaulle</strong><br>\nCharles de Gaulle was born in 1890 in Lille, France, to a family with a strong sense of patriotism and a history of military service. As a young man, he chose to follow in his family's footsteps and pursue a career in the military. He attended the prestigious Saint-Cyr Military Academy and graduated in 1912, beginning his service in the French Army.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War I Experience</strong><br>\nDuring World War I, de Gaulle served with distinction on the battlefields of Verdun and was taken prisoner by the Germans after being wounded. His repeated escape attempts from POW camps showed his commitment to France and his resilience. This period greatly influenced his future military strategies and leadership style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Interwar Period</strong><br>\nAfter the war, de Gaulle continued his military career and became known for his writings on tank warfare and military strategy. He argued for the use of armored divisions in modern warfare, a stance that was not widely accepted in the French military hierarchy at the time. His ideas, however, would later prove influential in World War II.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
