            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>World War II and the Fall of France</strong><br>\nWith the outbreak of World War II, de Gaulle led tanks in battle against the German invasion. After France's surrender in 1940, he refused to accept defeat. He fled to London, where he led the Free French Forces and became a symbol of French resistance, urging his countrymen to continue the fight against Nazi Germany.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Leader of Free France</strong><br>\nFrom London, de Gaulle broadcasted speeches to occupied France, encouraging resistance. He struggled to gain recognition for his provisional government but persisted in his efforts to liberate his country. His leadership helped to unify various French resistance groups and contributed to the Allied war effort.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Liberation of France and Aftermath</strong><br>\nDe Gaulle's determination was rewarded when he led the Free French in the liberation of Paris in August 1944. After the war, he became the head of the provisional government but resigned in 1946 due to political disagreements over the constitution of the Fourth Republic.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
