          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Fifth Republic</strong><br>\nDe Gaulle returned to power in 1958 during the Algerian crisis, establishing the Fifth Republic with a stronger presidency. He was elected President of France and worked to restore the nation's economy and prestige. Under his leadership, France pursued an independent foreign policy, distancing itself from U.S. and British influence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Policies and Impact</strong><br>\nAs president, de Gaulle sought to grant independence to French colonies, including Algeria. His policies often provoked controversy, but his vision was to make France a great power. He also worked to develop a nuclear deterrent, known as the \"force de frappe,\" ensuring France's defense and international standing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Retirement</strong><br>\nCharles de Gaulle retired in 1969 after a tenure marked by his efforts to rebuild France and shape its role in the world. His legacy includes the strong presidency of the Fifth Republic and his impact on French politics and international affairs. He passed away in 1970, leaving behind a nation significantly transformed by his leadership.</p>\n"
              },
